   160	3 Methodology
   161	Layerwise adaptive methods, such as LAMB, are observed to suffer from unstable and extreme
   162	trust ratios which degrades performance. This happens when the weight norm becomes too large
   163	compared to the gradient norm, leading to divergence during training. To solve this problem, we
   164	apply a clipping operation to the trust ratio (ratio between the L2-norm of weights and the L2-norm
   165	of the per layer gradients) by constraining it to be within a range of values between a predeﬁned set
   166	of upper and lower bounds. Speciﬁcally, at any timet,
   167	||w(i)||
   168	||∇L(w(i))|| =
   169	
   170	
   171	
   172	µ if ||w(i)||
   173	||∇L(w(i))|| <µ
   174	τ if ||w(i)||
   175	||∇L(w(i))|| >τ
   176	||w(i)||
   177	||∇L(w(i))|| if µ< ||w(i)||
   178	||∇L(w(i))|| <τ
   179	(3)
   180	whereµ∈ R+ andτ∈ R+ are the lower and upper bounds for all layers, whilew(i) and∇L(w(i))
   181	are the weights and gradients for layeri respectively. In our implementation, we set the lower bound
   182	τ = 0. Clipping the trust ratio prevents the weight update from exploding to huge values. As such,
   183	the degraded performance or training divergence caused by extreme and unstable trust ratios can be
   184	alleviated.
   185	The upper boundµ and lower boundτ in the clipping operation in Eq. 3 are hyperparameters that
   186	are manually tuned and are set as constant for all layers. To improve the ﬂexibility of training, one
   187	may consider using adaptive methods to decide the upper and lower bound for different layers during
   188	training. In our preliminary experiments, we tried an adaptive method from [15] to investigate this
   189	possibility. However, our empirical results show that such adaptive method performs not as well
   190	as manually deﬁned bounds, although with improvements over no clipping. We postulate that the
   191	dynamics of the upper and lower bound functions in [15] does not ﬁt the evolution of the trust ratio
   192	values during training, resulting in conﬂicting scenarios whereby clipping is performed on the trust
   193	ratio when it should not.
   194	3
   195	4 Experiments
   196	In this section, we will introduce the experiments we have done to validate the performance of the
   197	trust ratio clipping. Our experiments aim to answer the following questions:
   198	1. Can trust ratio clipping help with the task generalization and test performance?
   199	2. If trust ratio clipping works, what is the best or recommended trust ratio value that we should
   200	adopt?
   201	3. Does the trust ratio clipping work in a more complex image classiﬁcation task such as on
   202	ImageNet [1]?
   203	Following the three aforementioned questions, we divide the experiment section into three parts, each
   204	with individual experiments that address the respective research questions in detail.
   205	4.1 Image classiﬁcation on CIFAR10
   206	In this section, we aim to ﬁnd out whether applying trust ratio clipping results in test performance
   207	improvement compared against without clipping. We test our hypothesis on the CIFAR10 dataset
   208	[16] that contains 60000 32x32 colour images in 10 classes. Due to limited computation resources,
   209	we choose ResNet-18 [17] as our neural network model backbone. We set the learning rate to be
   210	1e-2, number of epochs to be 80 and compared the performance with and without trust ratio clipping
   211	on various batch sizes, ranging from 1000 to 3000. If trust ratio clipping is enabled, we clip the trust
   212	ratio to be less than 1.
   213	Figure 1: Image classiﬁcation task on CIFAR10 dataset [ 16]. We compare the scenarios with and
   214	without the trust ratio clipping. X-axis is the number of epochs and y-axis is the prediction accuracy
   215	in percentage. We conduct experiments on different batch sizes: 1000 (left), 2000 (middle) and 3000
   216	(right).
   217	Batch Size 1000 2000 3000
   218	Test Accuracy (clip) 87.71 87.3 86.29
   219	Test Accuracy (no clip) 85.68 86.61 85.41
   220	Table 1: Quantitative results for the test performance across different batch sizes.
   221	Figure 1 and Table 1 shows the evaluation results on CIFAR 10 dataset. All the three tasks with
   222	different batch sizes clearly indicate an improvement brought by trust ratio clipping on the ﬁnal
   223	testing performance. With batch size 1K, trust ratio clipping has the highest improvement of about
   224	2% for the testing accuracy and about 0.7% improvement for the other batch sizes. Therefore, we
   225	conclude that trust ratio clipping can improve on the task generalization and test performance.

