   144	In FedAvg, the central server updates the global model as follows:
   145	Global Update (FedAvg):wt+1 =wt +ηg ¯∆t,
   146	whereηg is the global learning rate. Vanilla FedAvg usesηg = 1, but in practice,ηg > 1 is often
   147	used to improve the convergence. FedAvg enjoys some favorable properties such as statelessness and
   148	communication efficiency, but it often suffers from slow convergence due to 1) the anisotropic nature
   149	of the local updates and 2) the heterogeneity of client data distributions. We call the former gradient
   150	heterogeneity and the latter client heterogeneity. Considering limited computational resources and
   151	communication bandwidth on clients, it is desirable to design an algorithm on a central server to
   152	overcome the above issues without changing the local training procedure.
   153	FedOpt To deal with the gradient heterogeneity, Reddi et al. [2021] have introduced a general
   154	framework called FedOpt. In this framework, the aggregated update ¯∆t is regarded as a pseudo-
   155	gradient and adaptive methods such as AdaGrad and Adam are used as a global optimizer. General
   156	update rule of FedOpt is given by
