   203	any adaptive optimizer (e.g. AMSG RAD (Reddi et al., 2019), ADABOUND (Luo et al., 2019)).
   204	While FEDOPT has intuitive beneﬁts over FEDAVG, it also raises a fundamental question: Can the
   205	negative of the average model difference∆t be used as a pseudo-gradient in general server optimizer
   206	updates? In this paper, we provide an afﬁrmative answer to this question by establishing a theoretical
   207	basis for FEDOPT. We will show that the use of the termSERVER OPT is justiﬁed, as we can guarantee
   208	convergence across a wide variety of server optimizers, including ADAGRAD , ADAM, and YOGI,
   209	thus developing principled adaptive optimizers for FL based on our framework.
   210	3 A DAPTIVE FEDERATED OPTIMIZATION
   211	In this section, we specialize FEDOPT to settings where SERVER OPT is an adaptive optimization
   212	method (one of ADAGRAD , YOGI or ADAM) and CLIENT OPT is SGD. By using adaptive methods
   213	(which generally require maintaining state) on the server and SGD on the clients, we ensure our
   214	methods have the same communication cost as FEDAVG and work in cross-device settings.
   215	Algorithm 2 provides pseudo-code for our methods. An alternate version using batched data and
