   380	The intuition behind “constant and drop” is straightfor-
   381	ward: a large learning rate is held constant for a reasonably
   382	long period to take advantage of faster convergence until it
   383	saturates, and then the learning rate is dropped by a constan t
   384	factor for more reﬁned training.
   385	We extend “constant and drop” to FedGM in Algorithm
   386	3, which we refer to as Multistage FedGM. In Multistage
   387	FedGM (Algorithm 3), each stage has length Ts (T =∑ S
   388	s=1 Ts), and has its triplet of stagewise hyperparameters
   389	{ηs, β s, ν s}S
   390	s=1. The convergence analysis in Sec 4.2 also
   391	applies to single-stage FedGM by S = 1.
   392	To our best knowledge, there is no prior work giving
   393	deﬁnitive theoretical guarantee or empirical performance s of
   394	any hyperparameter schedule in FL, especially considering
   395	multistage FedGM is an extremely ﬂexible framework that
   396	allows both learning rate and momentum factor to evolve.
