   227	Observing the success of trust ratio clipping in the previous experiments, we are curious about what
   228	is the best or recommended value to clip the trust ratio. We conduct another set of experiments on the
   229	CIFAR10 dataset [16] with different upper bound values of trust ratio clipping. We test the trust ratio
   230	values on four different scales: 1, 3, 5, 10.
   231	4
   232	Figure 2: Image classiﬁcation task on CIFAR10 dataset [16] with different trust ratio values. X-axis
   233	is the number of epochs and y-axis is the prediction accuracy in percentage.
   234	Figure 2 shows the evaluation results. Interestingly, we discover that all the conditions with trust
   235	ratio clipping outperform the no-clipping setup, while the ﬁnal testing performance is inversely
   236	proportional to the maximum clipping value. From the ﬁgure, clipping with max value 1 is the best,
   237	10 is the worst, and 3, 5 sits in between. Since trust ratio reﬂects the ratio between the magnitudes of
   238	the neural network weights and gradients, a possible explanation will be that drastic gradient updates
   239	on a stabilized weight parameter may jeopardize the generalization performance. This is indeed true,
   240	if the scale of the weight value attempts to stabilize, drastic changes (with trust ratio > 1) on the
   241	weight parameter take higher risks to downgrade the generalization performance. This observation is
   242	also consistent in the ﬁrst experiments in ﬁgure 1, where models with clipping only starts to surpass
   243	models without clipping at the later stage after the scale of the weight stabilized.
   244	An insight discovered here is to dynamically adjust the maximum trust ratio clipping value. At
   245	the beginning of the training, larger trust ratios should be allowed but it should be avoided after
   246	the weight scaling stabilizes, e.g. the converging phase, to prevent the risks of downgrading the
   247	generation performance.
   248	4.3 Image classiﬁcation on ImageNet
   249	In this section, we aim to ﬁnd out whether trust ratio clipping works in more complex image
   250	classiﬁcation tasks such as ImageNet [1]. ImageNet consists of 14 million real images with a total
   251	of 1000 classes. The original dataset occupies about 500G disk space, which exceeds beyond the
   252	capability of our computational resources and we have to conduct our experiments on the down-
   253	sampled ImageNet dataset on the scale of 64x64x3 per images. The batch size is also limited to the
   254	size of 400.
   255	The ﬁnal result is shown in Figure 3. From the ﬁgure, even with the more complex image classiﬁcation
   256	task on ImageNet, our proposed trust ratio clipping still helps with the generalization performance
   257	and outperforms the model without trust ratio clipping. Although, our model seems to over-ﬁt the
   258	training data with a much higher accuracy in the training dataset, our objective is not to achieve
   259	absolute performance in test dataset but to show the effectiveness of the trust ratio clipping over the
   260	model without trust ratio clipping.
