   452	Usingβ1 = 0 to save memory. By settingβ1 = 0, we can save on memory by replacing ˆm(t)
   453	1 by
   454	g(t). In this case, the memory cost of AdEMAMix is the same as for AdamW. We show in App. C.1.4
   455	(Fig. 15b) and App. C.1.7 that usingβ1 = 0 often works, at the cost of potentially less stable training.
   456	Computational overheads. Adding an additional EMA requires additional memory and compute.
   457	The added compute is negligible in comparison to what is required for the forward-backward, and
   458	has little impact over the total runtime as shown in Fig. 5a. Moreover—when considering larger
   459	distributed setups—it is worth noting that AdEMAMix is not increasing communication (gradient
   460	reduction) over Adam. Therefore, we expect the runtime overhead of AdEMAMix to shrink in those
   461	settings, as data movements occupy a larger fraction of the total runtime. A more significant overhead
   462	is in terms of memory whenβ1̸= 0, as AdEMAMix requires to allocate bothm1 andm2, which
   463	are of the same size as the model parameters θ. We believe this issue is of lesser consequences
   464	as Fully-Sharded-Data-Paralellism (Zhao et al., 2023, FSDP) can always be used to distribute the
   465	optimizer states across compute nodes. In the cases where memory remains an issue, one mitigation
   466	strategy could be to apply factorization strategies as in Shazeer & Stern (2018); Zhao et al. (2024).
   467	Hyperparameter sensitivity. While we introduce up to four new hyperparameters:α,β3,Tα, and
   468	Tβ3, in practice we always set Tα =Tβ3 =Tα,β3, and useTα,β3 =T in most cases. We show in
   469	App. C.1.4 thatTα andTβ3 should only be large enough to prevent instabilities early during training.
