   787	A veraging last two iterates inFedExP. Given the oscillating behavior of the iterates of FedExP,
   788	we ﬁnd that measuring progress onF (w) using the last iterate can be misleading. Motivated by this
   789	ﬁnding, we propose to set the ﬁnal model as the average of the last two iterates of FedExP. While
   790	the last iterate oscillates between regions that minimize the lossesF1(w) andF2(w) respectively,
   791	the behavior of the average of the last two iterates is more stable and proceeds along a globally low
   792	loss region. Interestingly, we ﬁnd that the beneﬁts of averaging the iterates of FedExP also extend
   793	to training neural networks with multiple clients in practical FL scenarios (see Appendix D.1). In
   794	practice, the number of iterates to average over could also be a hyperparameter for FedExP, but we
   795	ﬁnd that averaging the last two iterates works well, and we use this for our other experiments.
   796	6 E XPERIMENTS
   797	We evaluate the performance ofFedExP on synthetic and real FL tasks. For our synthetic experiment,
   798	we consider a distributed overparameterized linear regression problem. This experiment aligns most
   799	closely with our theory and allows us to carefully examine the performance of FedExP when (6)
   800	holds. For realistic FL tasks, we consider image classiﬁcation on the following datasets i) EMNIST
